chx:Document a doc:ReportSection ; rdfs:label "Strings and text editing for an advanced course: chapter 8 of the 3rd edition and today's Python"@en ; skos:definition "This document renews chapter 8 of the 3rd edition for an advanced course (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch08/research> ;
    doc:hasSection chx:S_StringLiterals, chx:S_StringAccess, chx:S_StringFormatting, chx:S_StringMethods, chx:S_CharactersAndBytes, chx:S_ClipboardAndPrograms .
chx:S_StringLiterals a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "String literals"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "String literals" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 1 ; doc:sectionOrder 1 ;
    skos:definition "How a program writes text in its source, with quotes, escape sequences and prefixes, opens Chapter 8 (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch08#StringLiterals> .
chx:S_StringQuoting a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "String quoting"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "String quoting" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 2 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_StringLiterals ;
    skos:definition "The chapter shows how to put a quotation mark or another hard-to-type character inside a string literal (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch08#StringQuoting> .
chx:S_QuoteStyles a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Quote styles"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Quote styles" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 3 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_StringQuoting ;
    skos:definition "A literal may begin and end with single or double quotes, so the chapter's literal about Alice's cat is written with double quotes and needs no escape, and 'Bob\\'s' with an escape has length 5 (Sweigart, 2025); the question about Howl's Moving Castle has the same answer, that the double quotes make the single quote ordinary; the reference adds that the choice of quote does not otherwise change how a literal is read (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch08#QuoteStyles> .
chx:S_EscapeSequences a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Escape sequences"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Escape sequences" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 4 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_StringQuoting ;
    skos:definition "Table 8-1 lists five escape sequences, the escapes for a single quote, a double quote, a tab, a newline and a backslash, and 'a\\tb' has three characters because the tab is one; 'line1\\nline2'.split('\\n') is ['line1', 'line2'] and '\\\\' has length 1 (Sweigart, 2025); the language reference lists more, among them \\a \\b \\f \\r \\v, \\ooo, \\xhh, \\N{name}, \\uxxxx and \\Uxxxxxxxx and a backslash at a line end that joins two lines, and '\\x41' and '\\101' are both 'A', '\\N{SNAKE}' is one character, which the chapter as read does not present (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch08#EscapeSequences> .
chx:S_StringPrefixes a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "String prefixes"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "String prefixes" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 5 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_StringQuoting ;
    skos:definition "The chapter uses the r prefix and the f prefix; the reference lists b, r, f, t and u, where u has no effect, and lets r combine with f, t and b, so type(b'a') is bytes while type(r'a') and type(u'a') are str and len(rb'\\n') is 2 (Sweigart, 2025) (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch08#StringPrefixes> .
chx:S_UnrecognizedEscapes a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Unrecognized escapes"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Unrecognized escapes" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 6 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_StringQuoting ;
    skos:definition "In '\\q' the backslash is kept, so list('\\\\q') is ['\\\\', 'q'], and compiling it draws a SyntaxWarning, which Python 3.12 introduced after a DeprecationWarning that began with Python 3.6, and a later version is to make it a SyntaxError; the chapter as read does not present it, and its advice to use raw strings for regular expressions avoids it (Sweigart, 2025) (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch08#UnrecognizedEscapes> .
chx:S_LiteralForms a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Literal forms"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Literal forms" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 7 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_StringLiterals ;
    skos:definition "A literal can be raw, spread over several lines or used as a comment (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch08#LiteralForms> .
chx:S_RawStrings a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Raw strings"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Raw strings" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 8 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_LiteralForms ;
    skos:definition "print(r'The file is in C:\\Users\\Alice\\Desktop') prints the path as written, r'Hello...\\n\\n...world!' prints the backslash pairs where the ordinary literal prints two line breaks, and len(r'\\n') is 2 while len('\\n') is 1 (Sweigart, 2025); the chapter names Windows paths and regular expressions as the uses (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch08#RawStrings> .
chx:S_MultilineStrings a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Multiline strings"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Multiline strings" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 9 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_LiteralForms ;
    skos:definition "Triple quotes keep the quotes, tabs and newlines between them: the feedcat.py letter to Alice as a triple-quoted string is equal to the one-line form with \\n, holds five newlines, and needs no escape for the single quote in Eve's (Sweigart, 2025); the indentation rules of blocks do not apply inside it, and eval of a triple-quoted Dear Al, blank line Bob gives the split ['Dear Al,', '', 'Bob']; a backslash right after the opening quotes drops the first newline (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch08#MultilineStrings> .
chx:S_MultilineComments a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Multiline comments"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Multiline comments" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 10 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_LiteralForms ;
    skos:definition "The chapter uses a multiline string as a comment before a function and as the text under def say_hello() (Sweigart, 2025); Python does not treat it as a comment but as a string, and the one that opens a function body is kept: executing a def whose body starts with a triple-quoted 'Says hi.' gives that text as the function's __doc__, which the chapter as read does not name (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch08#MultilineComments> .
chx:S_StringAccess a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "String access"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "String access" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 1 ; doc:sectionOrder 11 ;
    skos:definition "Strings are read like lists and tested with in (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch08#StringAccess> .
chx:S_Positions a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Positions"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Positions" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 12 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_StringAccess ;
    skos:definition "An index picks one character and a slice a run of characters (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch08#Positions> .
chx:S_StringIndexing a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "String indexing"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "String indexing" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 13 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Positions ;
    skos:definition "'Hello, world!' has 13 characters, index 0 is 'H', index 4 is 'o' and -1 is '!', a space before the bracket as in greeting [4] is allowed, and greeting[13] raises IndexError: string index out of range (Sweigart, 2025); there is no separate character type, since 'Hello'[0] is itself a str (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch08#StringIndexing> .
chx:S_StringSlicing a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "String slicing"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "String slicing" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 14 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Positions ;
    skos:definition "greeting[0:5] and greeting[:5] are 'Hello', greeting[7:-1] is 'world', greeting[7:] is 'world!' and the original stays 'Hello, world!' (Sweigart, 2025); a slice past the end is cut short, so greeting[7:99] is 'world!' and greeting[99:] is '', a step is allowed so greeting[::-1] is '!dlrow ,olleH', and s[:i] + s[i:] equals s for every i (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch08#StringSlicing> .
chx:S_StringProperties a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "String properties"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "String properties" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 15 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_StringAccess ;
    skos:definition "A string is immutable, and in tests for a substring (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch08#StringProperties> .
chx:S_StringImmutability a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "String immutability"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "String immutability" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 16 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_StringProperties ;
    skos:definition "name[7] = 'the' on 'Zophie a cat' raises TypeError: 'str' object does not support item assignment, and the changed text is built as name[0:7] + 'the' + name[8:12], 'Zophie the cat' (Sweigart, 2025); 'age ' + 4000 raises TypeError and 'age ' + str(4000) is 'age 4000', and the chapter's point that spam.upper() leaves spam alone is the same immutability (Sweigart, 2025) (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch08#StringImmutability> .
chx:S_InOperator a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "In operator"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "In operator" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 17 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_StringProperties ;
    skos:definition "'Hello' in 'Hello, World' is True, 'HELLO' in 'Hello, World' is False because capitals count, '' in 'spam' is True and 'cats' not in 'cats and dogs' is False (Sweigart, 2025); on a list the operator tests whole items, so 'gg' in 'eggs' is True while 'gg' in ['eggs'] is False (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch08#InOperator> .
chx:S_StringFormatting a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "String formatting"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "String formatting" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 1 ; doc:sectionOrder 18 ;
    skos:definition "Putting one string inside another is done with f-strings and their predecessors (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch08#StringFormatting> .
chx:S_FormatInterpolation a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Format interpolation"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Format interpolation" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 19 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_StringFormatting ;
    skos:definition "The chapter presents the f-string, and current Python adds specifiers, debug output and template strings (Sweigart, 2025) (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch08#FormatInterpolation> .
chx:S_FStrings a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "F strings"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "F strings" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 20 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_FormatInterpolation ;
    skos:definition "f'My name is {name}. I am {age} years old. In ten years I will be {age + 10}. {{name}}' with name 'Al' and age 4000 gives 'My name is Al. I am 4000 years old. In ten years I will be 4010. {name}' (Sweigart, 2025); the chapter says the content of the brackets is interpreted as if passed to str(), and the documentation says the value is formatted by format(), which for most values gives the same text (Python Software Foundation, 2026); f-strings came in Python 3.6 (Smith, 2015)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch08#FStrings> .
chx:S_FormatSpecifiers a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Format specifiers"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Format specifiers" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 21 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_FormatInterpolation ;
    skos:definition "f'{3.14159:.2f}|{42:>6}|{42:^6}|{42:06}|{1234567:,}|{255:#x}|{0.256:.1%}' gives '3.14|    42|  42  |000042|1,234,567|0xff|25.6%', and f'{s:>10}', f'{s:<10}', f'{s:^20}' and f'{s:*^20}' equal s.rjust(10), s.ljust(10), s.center(20) and s.center(20, '*'); a class with its own __format__ receives the text after the colon, so f'{c:.1f}' can differ from str(c), which is why the chapter's statement about str() holds only by default; the chapter as read does not present specifiers (Sweigart, 2025) (Python Software Foundation, 2026) (Talin, 2006)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch08#FormatSpecifiers> .
chx:S_DebugAndConversions a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Debug and conversions"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Debug and conversions" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 22 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_FormatInterpolation ;
    skos:definition "f'{s=}' with s = 'Al' gives the text s='Al', f'{n + 1 = }' keeps its spaces and gives 'n + 1 = 42', f'{s!r} {s!s}' gives the text 'Al' Al and f'{chr(233)!a}' gives the escaped form of the accented e; since Python 3.12 an f-string written in double quotes may hold a double-quoted dictionary key in a field, and a backslash inside a field is allowed, and the chapter as read does not present these (Sweigart, 2025) (Python Software Foundation, 2026) (Galindo Salgado et al., 2022)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch08#DebugAndConversions> .
chx:S_TemplateStrings a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Template strings"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Template strings" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 23 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_FormatInterpolation ;
    skos:definition "t'Hello {name}, you are {age:>6} years old.' has type Template, isinstance(t, str) is False, t.strings is ('Hello ', ', you are ', ' years old.') and its interpolations hold the expression, value, conversion and specifier, for example ('age', 4000, None, '>6'); a function that escapes the interpolated value of t'<p>{evil}</p>' gives <p>&lt;b&gt;x&lt;/b&gt;</p> where the f-string gives the raw tag; Python 3.13 rejects the t prefix with a SyntaxError, so these examples were run under Python 3.14.4 only and not in the page, and the chapter as read does not present them (Python Software Foundation, 2026) (Baker et al., 2024)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch08#TemplateStrings> .
chx:S_OlderStyles a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Older styles"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Older styles" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 24 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_StringFormatting ;
    skos:definition "Concatenation, the % operator and format() are the ways before the f-string (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch08#OlderStyles> .
chx:S_Concatenation a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Concatenation"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Concatenation" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 25 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_OlderStyles ;
    skos:definition "'Hello, my name is ' + name + '. I am ' + str(age) + ' years old.' gives 'Hello, my name is Al. I am 4000 years old.' and needs str(age) (Sweigart, 2025); two literals side by side are joined, 'Py' 'thon' being 'Python', and 3 * 'un' + 'ium' is 'unununium' (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch08#Concatenation> .
chx:S_PercentFormatting a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Percent formatting"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Percent formatting" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 26 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_OlderStyles ;
    skos:definition "'My name is %s. I am %s years old.' % (name, age) gives 'My name is Al. I am 4000 years old.' and '%05.1f|%-4s|%3d%%' % (3.14159, 'ab', 7) gives '003.1|ab  |  7%' (Sweigart, 2025); '%s' % (1, 2) raises TypeError because the tuple is read as two arguments for one specifier, '%s' % ((1, 2),) is '(1, 2)', and the documentation notes the quirks of this style and names f-strings, str.format() and string.Template (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch08#PercentFormatting> .
chx:S_FormatMethod a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Format method"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Format method" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 27 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_OlderStyles ;
    skos:definition "'{1} years ago, {0} was born and named {0}.'.format(name, age) gives '4000 years ago, Al was born and named Al.' and '{name} is {age}'.format(name='Al', age=4000) gives 'Al is 4000' (Sweigart, 2025); too few arguments raise IndexError: Replacement index 1 out of range for positional args tuple, and format() was specified for Python 3.0 (Talin, 2006) (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch08#FormatMethod> .
chx:S_StringMethods a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "String methods"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "String methods" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 1 ; doc:sectionOrder 28 ;
    skos:definition "A string method returns a new value and never changes the string it is called on (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch08#StringMethods> .
chx:S_TextCase a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Text case"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Text case" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 29 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_StringMethods ;
    skos:definition "Methods that convert or test the case of letters (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch08#TextCase> .
chx:S_ChangingCase a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Changing case"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Changing case" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 30 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_TextCase ;
    skos:definition "'Hello, world!'.upper() is 'HELLO, WORLD!' and .lower() is 'hello, world!', the string spam stays unchanged until spam = spam.upper() assigns the result, and 'great' == 'GREat'.lower() is True, which is the chapter's case-insensitive answer to the question How are you? (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch08#ChangingCase> .
chx:S_CaseTests a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Case tests"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Case tests" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 31 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_TextCase ;
    skos:definition "'Hello, world!'.isupper() and islower() are both False, 'HELLO'.isupper() and 'abc12345'.islower() are True, '12345'.islower() and '12345'.isupper() are False, and ''.islower() is False because at least one cased letter is needed (Sweigart, 2025) (Python Software Foundation, 2026); 'Hello'.upper().isupper() is True but '123'.upper().isupper() is False for the same reason (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch08#CaseTests> .
chx:S_CaseFolding a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Case folding"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Case folding" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 32 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_TextCase ;
    skos:definition "'stra\\xdfe'.lower() == 'strasse' is False and 'stra\\xdfe'.casefold() == 'strasse' is True, and len('stra\\xdfe') is 6 while len('stra\\xdfe'.upper()) is 7 because upper() gives STRASSE; the chapter compares with lower() and as read does not name casefold() (Sweigart, 2025) (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch08#CaseFolding> .
chx:S_MethodChaining a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Method chaining"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Method chaining" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 33 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_TextCase ;
    skos:definition "'Hello'.upper().lower().upper() is 'HELLO', 'HELLO'.lower().islower() is True and 'a-b'.upper().replace('-', '+') is 'A+B' (Sweigart, 2025); islower() returns a Boolean, so nothing can be chained after it (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch08#MethodChaining> .
chx:S_TextChecks a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Text checks"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Text checks" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 34 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_StringMethods ;
    skos:definition "Methods that test what kind of characters a string holds or how it begins and ends (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch08#TextChecks> .
chx:S_CharacterTests a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Character tests"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Character tests" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 35 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_TextChecks ;
    skos:definition "'hello'.isalpha() is True, 'hello123'.isalpha() is False, 'hello123'.isalnum() is True, '123'.isdecimal() is True, '    '.isspace() is True and 'This Is Title Case'.istitle() is True (Sweigart, 2025); every one of them is False for the empty string, and '4,000'.isalpha() is False (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch08#CharacterTests> .
chx:S_UnicodeDigits a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Unicode digits"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Unicode digits" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 36 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_TextChecks ;
    skos:definition "'\\xb2'.isdecimal() is False while '\\xb2'.isdigit() is True, '\\u0663'.isdecimal() is True and int('\\u0663') is 3, '\\u3000'.isspace() is True and an accented e is alphabetic but not ASCII, so 'caf\\xe9'.isalnum() is True and 'caf\\xe9'.isascii() is False; the chapter describes isspace() as spaces, tabs and newlines and as read does not mention the Unicode behaviour, so its password check with isalnum() accepts accented letters (Sweigart, 2025) (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch08#UnicodeDigits> .
chx:S_StartsEndsWith a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Starts ends with"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Starts ends with" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 37 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_TextChecks ;
    skos:definition "'Hello, world!'.startswith('Hello') and .endswith('world!') are True, 'abc123'.startswith('abcdef') and .endswith('12') are False (Sweigart, 2025); both accept a tuple, so 'notes.txt'.endswith(('.py', '.txt')) is True (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch08#StartsEndsWith> .
chx:S_JoiningAndSplittingText a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Joining and splitting text"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Joining and splitting text" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 38 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_StringMethods ;
    skos:definition "join() and split() convert between a list of strings and one string (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch08#JoiningAndSplittingText> .
chx:S_JoinMethod a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Join method"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Join method" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 39 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_JoiningAndSplittingText ;
    skos:definition "', '.join(['cats', 'rats', 'bats']) is 'cats, rats, bats' and 'ABC'.join(['My', 'name']) is 'MyABCname' (Sweigart, 2025); join accepts any iterable of strings, so '-'.join('abc') is 'a-b-c', and ', '.join([1, 2]) raises TypeError: sequence item 0: expected str instance, int found (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch08#JoinMethod> .
chx:S_SplitMethod a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Split method"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Split method" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 40 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_JoiningAndSplittingText ;
    skos:definition "'MyABCnameABCisABCSimon'.split('ABC') is ['My', 'name', 'is', 'Simon'] and 'My name is Simon'.split('m') is ['My na', 'e is Si', 'on'], and splitting the chapter's multiline letter on '\\n' gives seven items with an empty string for the blank line (Sweigart, 2025); '1,,2'.split(',') keeps the empty item and ''.split(',') is [''] (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch08#SplitMethod> .
chx:S_WhitespaceSplit a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Whitespace split"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Whitespace split" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 41 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_JoiningAndSplittingText ;
    skos:definition "'My name is Simon'.split() is ['My', 'name', 'is', 'Simon'] (Sweigart, 2025); with no argument runs of whitespace are one separator, so '  a \\t b\\n c  '.split() is ['a', 'b', 'c'] and ''.split() is [], while '  a  b '.split(' ') is ['', '', 'a', '', 'b', ''] (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch08#WhitespaceSplit> .
chx:S_SplitLines a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Split lines"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Split lines" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 42 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_JoiningAndSplittingText ;
    skos:definition "The chapter splits the clipboard text with split('\\n') (Sweigart, 2025); on 'one\\r\\ntwo\\nthree' that gives ['one\\r', 'two', 'three'] and splitlines() gives ['one', 'two', 'three'], and on 'a\\n\\nb\\n' split gives a trailing empty string and splitlines does not; splitlines() also cuts at \\x0b, \\x1c and \\u2028, and the chapter as read does not present it (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch08#SplitLines> .
chx:S_PartitionMethod a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Partition method"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Partition method" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 43 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_JoiningAndSplittingText ;
    skos:definition "'Monty Python'.partition(' ') is ('Monty', ' ', 'Python'), 'Monty Python'.partition('-') is ('Monty Python', '', '') and 'a.b.c'.rpartition('.') is ('a.b', '.', 'c'); the chapter as read does not present it (Sweigart, 2025) (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch08#PartitionMethod> .
chx:S_TextSpacing a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Text spacing"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Text spacing" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 44 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_StringMethods ;
    skos:definition "Padding and trimming change the width of a string by its ends (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch08#TextSpacing> .
chx:S_JustifyMethods a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Justify methods"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Justify methods" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 45 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_TextSpacing ;
    skos:definition "'Hello'.rjust(10) is five spaces and Hello, 'Hello'.ljust(10, '-') is 'Hello-----' and 'Hello'.center(20, '=') is '=======Hello========', with the extra padding on the right here, 7 left and 8 right (Sweigart, 2025); 'Hello'.rjust(20) has 15 spaces, which the chapter prints as 14, and 'Hello, World'.rjust(20) has 8, which it prints as 9, so both printed lines disagree with Python 3.14.4; a fill of two characters raises TypeError, a width no larger than the string changes nothing, and the f-string specifiers > < ^ give the same results (Sweigart, 2025) (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch08#JustifyMethods> .
chx:S_StripMethods a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Strip methods"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Strip methods" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 46 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_TextSpacing ;
    skos:definition "'    Hello, World    '.strip() is 'Hello, World', lstrip() keeps the right spaces, and 'SpamSpamBaconSpamEggsSpamSpam'.strip('ampS') is 'BaconSpamEggs' (Sweigart, 2025); the argument is a set of characters, so strip('mapS') and strip('Spam') give the same result (Sweigart, 2025) (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch08#StripMethods> .
chx:S_PrefixSuffixRemoval a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Prefix suffix removal"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Prefix suffix removal" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 47 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_TextSpacing ;
    skos:definition "'Arthur: three!'.lstrip('Arthur: ') is 'ee!' because the argument is a set of characters, while .removeprefix('Arthur: ') is 'three!', 'report.txt'.removesuffix('.txt') is 'report' and 'report.txt'.removesuffix('.py') is unchanged; the chapter as read does not present them (Python Software Foundation, 2026) (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch08#PrefixSuffixRemoval> .
chx:S_Searching a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Searching"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Searching" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 48 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_StringMethods ;
    skos:definition "Finding and replacing text inside a string, which the chapter leaves to the course (Sweigart, 2025) (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch08#Searching> .
chx:S_FindAndCount a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Find and count"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Find and count" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 49 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Searching ;
    skos:definition "'spam eggs spam'.find('spam') is 0, find('spam', 1) is 10, 'spam'.find('x') is -1, 'spam eggs spam'.count('spam') is 2, 'aaaa'.count('aa') is 2 because matches do not overlap and 'spam'.count('') is 5; the chapter as read does not present them (Sweigart, 2025) (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch08#FindAndCount> .
chx:S_ReplaceMethod a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Replace method"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Replace method" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 50 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Searching ;
    skos:definition "'spam, spam, spam'.replace('spam', 'eggs') is 'eggs, eggs, eggs', with a count of 1 it is 'eggs, spam, spam', and 'abc'.replace('b', '') is 'ac'; count can be a keyword since Python 3.13, and the chapter as read does not present it (Sweigart, 2025) (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch08#ReplaceMethod> .
chx:S_CharactersAndBytes a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Characters and bytes"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Characters and bytes" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 1 ; doc:sectionOrder 51 ;
    skos:definition "How text becomes numbers and bytes (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch08#CharactersAndBytes> .
chx:S_CodePointConversion a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Code point conversion"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Code point conversion" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 52 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_CharactersAndBytes ;
    skos:definition "Every character has a number in Unicode (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch08#CodePointConversion> .
chx:S_OrdAndChr a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Ord and chr"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Ord and chr" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 53 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_CodePointConversion ;
    skos:definition "ord('A') is 65, ord('4') is 52, ord('!') is 33, chr(65) is 'A', ord('A') < ord('B') is True and chr(ord('A') + 1) is 'B' (Sweigart, 2025); ord of the euro sign is 8364, ord of the snake emoji is 128013, chr() accepts only 0 to 0x10FFFF and raises ValueError above it, and ord('ab') raises TypeError (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch08#OrdAndChr> .
chx:S_TextEncodings a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Text encodings"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Text encodings" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 54 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_CharactersAndBytes ;
    skos:definition "Encoding and decoding turn text into bytes and back (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch08#TextEncodings> .
chx:S_Utf8Encoding a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Utf8 encoding"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Utf8 encoding" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 55 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_TextEncodings ;
    skos:definition "'caf\\xe9'.encode('utf-8') is b'caf\\xc3\\xa9', so four characters are five bytes and decoding returns the original; the snake emoji is one character and four bytes in UTF-8 and in UTF-16 (Python Software Foundation, 2026); the chapter says 'utf-8' is the correct choice 99 percent of the time and points to Ned Batchelder's talk and a Computerphile video (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch08#Utf8Encoding> .
chx:S_EncodeDecodeErrors a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Encode decode errors"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Encode decode errors" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 56 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_TextEncodings ;
    skos:definition "Encoding 'caf\\xe9' as ascii raises UnicodeEncodeError: 'ascii' codec can't encode character '\\xe9' in position 3, decoding b'caf\\xc3' as utf-8 raises UnicodeDecodeError, errors='replace' gives b'caf?', 'backslashreplace' gives b'caf\\\\xe9' and 'ignore' gives b'caf', and decoding UTF-8 bytes as latin-1 succeeds but gives the wrong text; the chapter as read does not present them (Python Software Foundation, 2026) (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch08#EncodeDecodeErrors> .
chx:S_FileEncoding a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "File encoding"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "File encoding" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 57 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_TextEncodings ;
    skos:definition "Writing 'caf\\xe9' through a text wrapper with encoding='utf-8' leaves b'caf\\xc3\\xa9'; open() without encoding uses the locale encoding in Python 3.14.4, so a file written as UTF-8 was read back in a child process with the C locale and UTF-8 mode off under the ascii codec, which raised UnicodeDecodeError, while encoding='utf-8' read it correctly; PEP 686 makes UTF-8 mode the default from Python 3.15 (Python Software Foundation, 2026) (Inada, 2022); the chapter as read mentions files only as the place where encoding happens (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch08#FileEncoding> .
chx:S_ClipboardAndPrograms a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Clipboard and programs"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Clipboard and programs" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 1 ; doc:sectionOrder 58 ;
    skos:definition "The chapter's programs work on text from the clipboard and from the user (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch08#ClipboardAndPrograms> .
chx:S_ClipboardPrograms a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Clipboard programs"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Clipboard programs" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 59 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_ClipboardAndPrograms ;
    skos:definition "The clipboard lets a program receive and hand over large text (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch08#ClipboardPrograms> .
chx:S_ClipboardModule a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Clipboard module"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Clipboard module" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 60 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_ClipboardPrograms ;
    skos:definition "pyperclip.copy('Hello, world!') and pyperclip.paste() send text to and receive text from the clipboard, and the module does not come with Python, so importing it on the build machine raised ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'pyperclip' (Sweigart, 2025); the clipboard itself was not executed here, and the chapter's programs were run against a stand-in module that keeps the text in a variable (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch08#ClipboardModule> .
chx:S_ProjectPrograms a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Project programs"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Project programs" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 61 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_ClipboardAndPrograms ;
    skos:definition "The chapter's clipboard projects, its Pig Latin program and its practice program (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch08#ProjectPrograms> .
chx:S_BulletPointAdder a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Bullet point adder"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Bullet point adder" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 62 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_ProjectPrograms ;
    skos:definition "Run unchanged against a stand-in clipboard, bulletPointAdder.py turns the four lines from Lists of animals to Lists of cultivars into the four lines with a star and a space in front, as printed; it loops with range(len(lines)) and joins with '\\n'.join(lines), and its step 2 listing ends with pyperclip.copy(text) on the unchanged text, which step 3 corrects by joining the lines (Sweigart, 2025); with Windows line ends split('\\n') leaves a carriage return in every line and a trailing newline gives a final line holding only a star, where splitlines() does not (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch08#BulletPointAdder> .
chx:S_AlternatingText a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Alternating text"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Alternating text" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 63 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_ProjectPrograms ;
    skos:definition "Run unchanged against a stand-in clipboard, alternatingText.py turns 'Hello, world!' into 'hElLo, WoRlD!' by switching on every character, spaces included; on the chapter's sentence the result begins iF YoU CoPy sOmE TeXt and ends with tHiS:, whereas the chapter prints a version that ends with ThIs: and has a line break inside, so its printed output was not reproduced and the cause was not established (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch08#AlternatingText> .
chx:S_InputValidation a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Input validation"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Input validation" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 64 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_ProjectPrograms ;
    skos:definition "validateInput.py printed exactly the prompts the chapter shows for the inputs forty two, 42, secr3t! and secr3t: it rejected the first, accepted the second, rejected the third and accepted the fourth (Sweigart, 2025); because isdecimal() accepts Arabic-Indic digits and isalnum() accepts accented letters, an age check with int() and a letters-and-numbers rule need isascii() where only ASCII is meant (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch08#InputValidation> .
chx:S_PigLatin a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Pig latin"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Pig latin" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 65 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_ProjectPrograms ;
    skos:definition "Run unchanged, pigLat.py prints Ymay amenay isyay ALYAY EIGARTSWAY andyay Iyay amyay 4,000 yearsyay oldyay. for the chapter's message, whose split() gives eleven words; it treats y as a vowel, so years becomes yearsyay and rhythm becomes ythmrhay, it loses the capital of a word such as It's because istitle() is False for it, giving it'syay, and it treats an accented e as a consonant, so a word starting with one is moved, which the chapter as read does not say; the walk-through line with += shows seven exclamation marks for Wait!!! where the listing keeps three (Sweigart, 2025) (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch08#PigLatin> .
chx:S_TablePrinter a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Table printer"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Table printer" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 66 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_ProjectPrograms ;
    skos:definition "The chapter asks for printTable(tableData) for three lists of four strings with right-justified columns, and a solution that finds the widths [8, 5, 5] with a loop and prints ' ' + item.rjust(width) for each cell prints the four lines of the chapter's table exactly; the chapter ends with 10 practice questions and this one practice program (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch08#TablePrinter> .
